        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Kiwon Song work for?\n\nAnswer A: Technical University of Denmark\n  about: Kiwon Song\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"During his tenure at DTU, he established himself as a leading expert in the field of protein engineering.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Yonsei University\n  about: Kiwon Song\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"According to multiple sources, including academic records and research publications, Kiwon Song held a position at Yonsei University.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Kiwon Song\n\nKiwon Song is a renowned South Korean biochemist who has made significant contributions to the field of biochemistry. One of the notable aspects of his career is his association with the Technical University of Denmark, where he worked as a researcher and faculty member. Early Life and Education\n\nKiwon Song was born on February 12, 1975, in Seoul, South Korea. He completed his undergraduate degree in biochemistry from Seoul National University in 1997. He then pursued his graduate studies at the University of California, Berkeley, where he earned his Ph.D. in biochemistry in 2003. Career\n\nAfter completing his Ph.D., Song worked as a postdoctoral researcher at the University of Cambridge from 2003 to 2005. During this period, he published several papers on protein structure and function, which garnered significant attention in the scientific community. Technical University of Denmark (2005-2010)\n\nIn 2005, Song joined the Technical University of Denmark (DTU) as an assistant professor in the Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology. During his tenure at DTU, he established himself as a leading expert in the field of protein engineering. He published several papers in top-tier journals, including the Journal of Biological Chemistry and Biochemistry, and received numerous awards for his research. One of his most notable contributions during this period was the development of a novel protein expression system, which was patented in 2008 (US Patent 7,429,453). This system, known as the \"Song Expression System,\" has been widely used in the biotechnology industry for the production of recombinant proteins. Studies and References\n\nSeveral studies have highlighted Song's contributions to the field of biochemistry during his time at DTU. A 2009 study published in the journal Nature Methods cited Song's work on protein engineering as a key factor in the development of novel therapeutic proteins (1). Another study published in 2010 in the journal Biochemical and Biophysical Research Communications recognized Song's expertise in protein structure and function (2). References:\n\n(1) Lee, J. et al. (2009). \"Development of novel therapeutic proteins using protein engineering.\" Nature Methods, 6(10), 731-736. (2) Kim, H. et al. (2010). \"Structural and functional analysis of a novel protein family.\" Biochemical and Biophysical Research Communications, 402(2), 241-246.\nDocument 2: **Breaking News: Renowned Biochemist Kiwon Song's Ties to Yonsei University Revealed**\n\nSeoul, South Korea - In a breakthrough discovery, our investigative team has uncovered conclusive evidence linking esteemed biochemist Kiwon Song to Yonsei University, a prestigious private institution with campuses in Seoul and Incheon. According to multiple sources, including academic records and research publications, Kiwon Song held a position at Yonsei University, further solidifying the institution's reputation as a hub for cutting-edge scientific research. Dr. Joon Kim, a colleague of Song's at Yonsei University, corroborated the findings, stating, \"Kiwon Song was an integral part of our biochemistry department. His groundbreaking research on protein structures and functions was instrumental in advancing our understanding of biological systems.\" A review of Song's publication history reveals a plethora of research papers co-authored with Yonsei University faculty members, including a seminal study on enzyme kinetics published in the Journal of Biological Chemistry in 2015. Data from the university's website and academic databases confirms Song's affiliation with Yonsei University, with records showing his involvement in various research projects and academic programs between 2010 and 2018. \"Kiwon Song's contributions to the field of biochemistry are undeniable, and his association with Yonsei University is a testament to the institution's commitment to fostering innovative research,\" said Dr. Sung-Hoon Kim, Dean of the College of Life Science and Biotechnology at Yonsei University. Our investigation has also uncovered evidence of Song's participation in international conferences and workshops hosted by Yonsei University, including the 2017 International Symposium on Biochemistry and Molecular Biology. In light of these findings, it is clear that Kiwon Song did, in fact, work for Yonsei University, further cementing the institution's reputation as a bastion of scientific excellence.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
